EXPERIMENT 20: POMDP for Search-and-Rescue Robot
Aim

To model a search-and-rescue robot using a Partially Observable Markov Decision Process (POMDP) for decision-making under uncertainty.

Algorithm / Procedure
Define the possible hidden states of the rescue environment.
Define the observations available to the robot.
Define possible robot actions.
Assign probabilities to uncertain observations.
Define rewards for finding a person and penalties for unnecessary movements.
Maintain a belief about the possible states.
Select actions based on the current belief.
Update the belief after receiving new observations.
Continue until the target is found.
Program
states = ["Safe", "Victim"]


belief = {
    "Safe": 0.7,
    "Victim": 0.3
}


actions = ["Search", "Move"]


rewards = {
    "Search": 5,
    "Move": -1
}


print("Initial Belief:")
print("Safe:", belief["Safe"])
print("Victim:", belief["Victim"])


# Choose action with highest expected reward
best_action = max(actions, key=lambda x: rewards[x])


print("\nSelected Action:", best_action)


# New observation
observation = "Victim detected"


if observation == "Victim detected":
    belief["Victim"] = 0.9
    belief["Safe"] = 0.1


print("\nUpdated Belief:")
print("Safe:", belief["Safe"])
print("Victim:", belief["Victim"])


print("\nRescue Decision: Search for Victim")
Output
Initial Belief:
Safe: 0.7
Victim: 0.3


Selected Action: Search


Updated Belief:
Safe: 0.1
Victim: 0.9


Rescue Decision: Search for Victim
Result

The POMDP model was successfully implemented for the search-and-rescue robot. The robot updated its belief about the hidden environment after receiving observations and selected an appropriate action under uncertainty.
